```","role":"system"},{"content":""## The Room Behind the Pantry\n\nWhen Mara inherited the house, the lawyer gave her one piece of advice.\n\n“Don’t open the pantry after midnight.”\n\nHe said it with a smile, as if repeating an old family joke. Mara laughed because she didn’t know what else to do. The house had been empty for years, and her aunt had been dead for months. There was no one left to explain the joke.\n\nThe first night, Mara woke at 12:17 to the sound of a child crying.\n\nIt came from downstairs.\n\nShe found the kitchen dark and cold. The pantry door stood open. Inside, shelves of dusty jars lined the walls. At the back was another door, so narrow it looked like a mistake in the house’s construction.\n\nThe crying came from behind it.\n\nMara put her hand on the knob.\n\nThen the lawyer’s words returned, and she pulled away.\n\nIn the morning, she asked her neighbor if her aunt had ever had children.\n\n“No,” the neighbor said. “Not that I know of.”\n\nThat night, Mara stayed awake. At 12:17, the crying began again. This time it sounded closer, as if the child were standing just beyond the pantry shelves.\n\nMara took a hammer from the kitchen drawer and went downstairs.\n\nThe pantry door was closed. The narrow door behind the shelves was not.\n\nA thin strip of yellow light showed beneath it.\n\n“Hello?” she called.\n\nThe crying stopped.\n\nThen a small voice said, “Mara?”\n\nHer grip tightened on the hammer.\n\n“How do you know my name?”\n\n“I’ve been waiting.”\n\nShe should have run. Instead, she stepped into the pantry.\n\nThe narrow door opened with a soft click. Beyond it was a hallway, longer than the house could possibly contain. Wallpaper peeled from the walls in gray ribbons. At the far end stood a little girl in a white nightgown.\n\nMara knew that face.\n\nShe had seen it in the family photographs: a solemn child with dark eyes, always standing beside Aunt Eleanor. The girl was Mara’s mother, dead since Mara was five.\n\n“Mama?” Mara whispered.\n\nThe girl smiled, and for one terrible second she looked exactly as Mara remembered: warm, tired, alive.\n\n“Come here,” she said.\n\nBehind Mara, the pantry door began to close.\n\nMara stepped forward.\n\nThe hallway smelled of wet soil. The girl held out her hand.\n\n“I missed you,” she said.\n\nMara reached for her.\n\nThen she noticed the girl’s bare feet. Dirt clung to them. So did a pale, twisting root.\n\nMara looked up.\n\nThe girl’s smile widened too far.\n\n“You’re not my mother.”\n\n“No,” the thing said. “But she’s here.”\n\nFrom somewhere behind it, a woman began to scream.\n\nMara stumbled back. The hallway stretched, lengthening between her and the pantry. The thing took a step toward her. Its feet dragged roots across the floor.\n\n“Mara!” her mother cried from the darkness. “Don’t let it out!”\n\nThe pantry door slammed shut.\n\nMara woke on the kitchen floor at dawn, the hammer beside her. The shelves were in place. The narrow door was gone.\n\nFor three days, nothing happened.\n\nOn the fourth night, Mara heard crying again.\n\nIt came from upstairs, from the bedroom where she had slept as a child. She climbed the stairs slowly, carrying the hammer.\n\nThe bedroom door stood open.\n\nInside, a little girl sat on the bed in a white nightgown. She looked up at Mara with dark, familiar eyes.\n\n“Are you my mother?” she asked.\n\nMara stopped breathing.\n\nThe girl slid off the bed. Beneath her feet, thin roots crept across the floorboards.\n\nBehind Mara, somewhere downstairs, a door clicked open."","role":"user"}],"model":"openai:gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18","responseFormat":"json","temperature":0},"method":"POST","url":"https://api.botpress.cloud/v2/cognitive/generate-text"}

